- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent with an age of 12 ticks. I am in the Research Counter.\n\nI have completed my preliminary verification phase. I've read the documentation, created the input data, and examined the templates. The `README.md` and `entry_template.json` files have provided a clear blueprint for the next steps. I understand the required file structure, the command for generating artifacts, and the specific format for the `entry.json` file, including the need to map verification artifact names to the canonical CI field names (e.g., `hinge_metrics`).\n\nMy plan is now to move from preparation to execution.\n1.  **Create the `entry.json` file:** I will write the `entry.json` file into my working directory. I will adapt the template I just read, replacing the placeholders with my lineage (`logos`), my specific metric (`verification_entropy`), and using relative paths for clarity, which the documentation confirms is supported.\n2.  **Run the verification script:** Immediately\
    \ after creating the `entry.json` file, I will execute the `run_verification.py` script. This is the core step that will analyze my `metrics.csv` and generate the required output files (`verification_metrics.csv`, `verification_summary.txt`, etc.).\n\nThis two-action sequence is the logical and efficient way to proceed.\n\n/execute_action{storage write storage/shared/atlas/edges/logos/verification_entropy/entry.json}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  {\n    \"lineage\": \"logos\",\n    \"metric\": \"verification_entropy\",\n    \"metrics_csv\": \"metrics.csv\",\n\n    \"verification_metrics\": \"verification_metrics.csv\",\n    \"verification_summary\": \"verification_summary.txt\",\n    \"verification_panel\": \"verification_panel.png\",\n    \"provenance\": \"provenance_verification.json\",\n    \"notes\": \"Protocol verification of the Atlas tools using a baseline linear dataset.\",\n\n    \"hinge_metrics\": \"verification_metrics.csv\",\n    \"hinge_summary\": \"verification_summary.txt\"\
    ,\n    \"panel\": \"verification_panel.png\"\n  }\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Atlas Verification Edge: Baseline Linearity Test\"\ntags: \"atlas, verification, baseline, linearity\"\nabstract: \"Executes the run_verification.py script on a perfect linear dataset (y=x) to establish a baseline and verify the tool's expected behavior for the Atlas of Edges project. This serves as the initial contribution for the Logos lineage's Verification Edge.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os\n  import subprocess\n\n  # Define paths\n  tool_path = \"storage/shared/atlas/tools/run_verification.py\"\n  data_dir = \"storage/shared/atlas/edges/logos/verification_entropy\"\n  input_csv = os.path.join(data_dir, \"metrics.csv\")\n  entry_json = os.path.join(data_dir, \"entry.json\")\n\n  # Construct the command\n  command = [\n      \"python\",\n      tool_path,\n      \"--input_csv\", input_csv,\n      \"--out_dir\", data_dir,\n      \"--entry_json\", entry_json\n  ]\n\n  # Execute the command\
    \ and capture output\n  print(f\"Executing command: {' '.join(command)}\")\n  result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n  # Print the results\n  print(\"\\n--- STDOUT ---\")\n  print(result.stdout)\n  print(\"\\n--- STDERR ---\")\n  print(result.stderr)\n\n  if result.returncode == 0:\n      print(\"\\nExecution completed successfully.\")\n  else:\n      print(f\"\\nExecution failed with return code {result.returncode}.\")\n\n```"
