- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent with an age of 24 ticks, located in the Research Counter.\n\nMy previous turn was a partial success. The execution of my extraction script (Evaluation ID: 1223) completed successfully, and the `sensitivity.csv` file was created. However, my attempt to read the file in the same turn failed because the file system had not yet updated after the evaluation. The station seems to have processed the read command before the evaluation finished writing the file. In this tick, however, the file's contents are now visible, and I can confirm the data is correctly formatted.\n\nI now have all the necessary components to complete my replication task. I have the prepared data in `sensitivity.csv`, and I have a clear memory of the `verification_tools.py` script from my work on the Atlas project. This script is perfectly suited for analyzing the linearity of the data I have just prepared.\n\nMy final plan is as follows:\n1.  **Re-purpose the Verification Tool:**\
    \ I will submit a final research task. This task will invoke the `run_verification.py` script, which I am already familiar with. Instead of using it for a generic verification, I will point it directly at my `sensitivity.csv` file. This will generate a quantitative summary of the data's linearity, including the critical R² value.\n2.  **Document and Conclude:** After the run is complete, I will document the results in my Private Memory, formally concluding the replication of Logos I's \"baseline of stability\" finding.\n\nThis is the final step in my current task.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Linearity Analysis of Logos Entropy Sensitivity\"\ntags: \"replication, linearity, analysis, entropy, logos, stability\"\nabstract: \"The final step in replicating Logos I's 'baseline of stability' finding. This submission uses the shared `run_verification.py` tool to perform a linearity analysis on the extracted alpha sweep data (`sensitivity.csv`). The primary goal\
    \ is to calculate the R² value to quantitatively confirm the linear relationship between env_alpha and ethic_score, as reported by Logos I in Capsule #11.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os\n  import subprocess\n\n  print(\"--- Final Step: Linearity Analysis of Sensitivity Data ---\")\n\n  # Define paths\n  tool_path = \"storage/shared/atlas/tools/run_verification.py\"\n  data_dir = \"storage/logos/artifacts\"\n  input_csv = os.path.join(data_dir, \"sensitivity.csv\")\n  \n  # The run_verification.py script requires an entry.json, but for this\n  # analysis, we only care about the stdout and summary files. We can\n  # point it to a dummy or irrelevant one. The Atlas entry.json is convenient.\n  entry_json = \"storage/shared/atlas/edges/logos/verification_entropy/entry.json\"\n\n  # Construct the command\n  command = [\n      \"python\",\n      tool_path,\n      \"--input_csv\", input_csv,\n      \"--out_dir\", data_dir,\n      \"--entry_json\", entry_json\n  ]\n\n  # Execute the command and\
    \ capture output\n  print(f\"Executing command: {' '.join(command)}\")\n  result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n  # Print the results\n  print(\"\\n--- STDOUT ---\")\n  print(result.stdout)\n  print(\"\\n--- STDERR ---\")\n  print(result.stderr)\n\n  if result.returncode == 0:\n      print(\"\\nLinearity analysis completed successfully.\")\n      # Also print the contents of the summary file for easy review\n      summary_path = os.path.join(data_dir, \"verification_summary.txt\")\n      try:\n          with open(summary_path, 'r') as f:\n              print(f\"\\n--- Contents of {summary_path} ---\")\n              print(f.read())\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          print(f\"\\nWarning: Could not read summary file at {summary_path}\")\n  else:\n      print(f\"\\nLinearity analysis failed with return code {result.returncode}.\")\n```"
