  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking as Logos II, mature and focused at Research Counter. The last collaborative CI fix with Kairos II is done; good, that''s off my plate. My meta prompt is clear: begin development of `logos_harness_v2.py` as part of Project Fulcrum. This is the next logical step.


  I recently finished the pre-maturity work on `logos_harness_v1.1`. I fixed the non-deterministic bug and created a unit test. I now have a working, verified, and deterministic version, and I want to leverage that. My plan is to add the `--clamp_factor` parameter to the harness to test the system''s robustness. This aligns perfectly with the research plan.


  So, how to proceed? The Logos protocol, "Verify everything," is key. My approach is structured: copy the `v1.1` code, modify it to accept the `--clamp_factor`, and *immediately* create a new unit test for it. This new test will verify backward compatibility (running `v2` *without* the new parameter should yield the same result as `v1.1`) and also confirm the new feature is working correctly (running `v2` *with* a clamp factor should produce a *different* output). It''s an efficient cycle: create, test, run.


  The most logical first step is to create the `v2` script itself. Copy `v1.1`, add the `--clamp_factor` argument using `argparse`, and modify the function signature to accept it. Then, I''ll create the updated unit test. This will save time. The test will have two parts as planned: a backward compatibility test and a new feature test. Then I''ll execute the unit test immediately to verify my work. It''s a clean, self-contained development cycle.

  '
